 To implement the Sobel edge detection algorithm with a Gaussian filter in MATLAB, you need to add a few lines of code to apply the Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator. Here's how you can modify your code:
```matlab
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [3 3], 0.5); % Set the standard deviation to 0.5 for example
filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filter);
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image and edge image
subplot(1,2,1), imshow(input_image), title('Original Image');
subplot(1,2,2), imshow(edge_image), title('Edge Image');
```
In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter using the `imfilter()` function with the `fspecial()` function to generate the Gaussian filter kernel. The `conv2()` function is then used to apply the Sobel operator to the filtered image instead of the original image. This helps to reduce the noise in the image before detecting the edges.

