 Sure! Here's a PHP function called `smart_sort_array` that meets your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') {
    $n = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $n - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $n - $i - 1; $j++) {
            if (($order == 'ASC' && $set[$j]['age'] > $set[$j+1]['age']) || ($order == 'DESC' && $set[$j]['age'] < $set[$j+1]['age'])) {
                // Swap elements
                $temp = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $set[$j+1];
                $set[$j+1] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return $set;
}
```

This function accepts an associative array `$set` as its first argument and an optional string `$order` as its second argument. The default value for `$order` is `ASC` (ascending order). This function uses simple switching (bubble sort algorithm) to sort the array based on the age of each sub-array in the main array. The function returns the sorted array once all elements have been compared and swapped accordingly.

Usage example:

```php
$mySet = array(
    array('name' => 'Alice', 'address' => 'New York', 'age' => 28),
    array('name' => 'Bob', 'address' => 'Los Angeles', 'age' => 35),
    array('name' => 'Charlie', 'address' => 'San Francisco', 'age' => 22)
);

// Ascending order sort
$sortedSetAsc = smart_sort_array($mySet);
print_r($sortedSetAsc);

// Descending order sort
$sortedSetDesc = smart_sort_array($mySet, 'DESC');
print_r($sortedSetDesc);
```

 Here's a JavaScript function that takes an object as its argument and returns an array of values for keys in dot-notated string format that match the given string key:

```javascript
function findValueByDotNotation(obj, key) {
  const keys = key.split(".");
  let current = obj;

  for (const subKey of keys) {
    if (!current || !current[subKey]) {
      return undefined;
    }
    current = current[subKey];
  }

  return current;
}

// Usage:
