 I'd be happy to help you write a C function that takes an array of pointers to strings (argv) from the command line and converts it into a single C string. Here's an example implementation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h> // For malloc()
#include <string.h> // For strcat()

// Function to concatenate all argv strings into one C string
char *convert_argv_to_cstring(int argc, char **argv) {
    int total_length = 0;
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        total_length += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // +1 for space between strings
    }

    char *result = (char *) malloc((total_length + 1) * sizeof(char)); // +1 for null terminator

    strcpy(result, "");

    for (i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
        strcat(result, argv[i]);
        if (i < argc - 1) {
            strcat(result, " "); // Add space between strings, except for last one
        }
    }

    return result;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *str = convert_argv_to_cstring(argc, argv);
    printf("Combined C string: %s\n", str);
    free(str);
    return 0;
}
```

This function calculates the total length required for the new C string, then allocates memory for it using `malloc()`. It then iterates through each `argv` string, appending it to the result using `strcat()`, with a space added between strings except for the last one. Finally, it returns the combined C string. The main function demonstrates how to use this conversion function in a simple program.

