 Here's an example of a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` environment variable and behaves accordingly:

```typescript
import { createParamDecorator, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

export const NodeEnvCheck = () => {
  return createParamDecorator(
    (data: unknown, ctx: ExecutionContext) => {
      const req = ctx.switchToHttp().getRequest();
      const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

      // If NODE_ENV is set to 'true', call the next middleware function
      if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
        return req.next();
      }

      // Otherwise, return false
      return false;
    },
  );
};
```

This decorator uses the `createParamDecorator` function provided by Nest.js to define a new decorator named `NodeEnvCheck`. It takes no arguments and returns a function that can be used as a middleware. The function checks the `NODE_ENV` environment variable and either calls the next middleware function or returns false.

To use this decorator, you would apply it to a middleware function using the `@ApplyDecorators` or `@UseGuards` decorators:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, ApplyDecorators, UseGuards } from '@nestjs/common';
import { NodeEnvCheck } from './node-env.decorator';

@Controller('example')
export class ExampleController {
  @Get('/')
  @ApplyDecorators(NodeEnvCheck())
  getHello(@Request() req): string {
    return req.app.get('hello');
  }
}
```

In this example, the `getHello` method is decorated with both the `@Get` decorator and the `NodeEnvCheck` decorator. When this route is accessed, the `NodeEnvCheck` middleware will be called first. If `NODE_ENV` is set to 'true', control will be passed to the `getHello` method. If `NODE_ENV` is false, the request will not continue and no further middleware or controller logic will be executed.

