 To prevent your planets from being generated too close to each other, you can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function by implementing a rejection mechanism when a newly generated planet is too close to an existing one. Instead of using the `while` loop, you can use a `do-while` loop or keep generating planets until the condition is met. Here's an updated version of the function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempt = 0;

  do {
    attempt++;
    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

  } while (tooClose && attempt < 100);

  if (attempt >= 100) {
    throw new Error("Failed to place enough planets.");
  }

  planets.push(planet);
  return planets;
};
```

This change will ensure that the planets are placed at sufficient distances from each other, preventing the generation of too many planets within a short distance. However, if you cannot place the required number of planets within the given boundary, you will need to consider increasing the boundary size or decreasing the number of planets.

